# 27. Queueable Limits — bahut important

Ab interview/development perspective se limits samjho.

## Single transaction mein Queueable jobs

Normally ek transaction se maximum:

```text
50 Queueable jobs
```

enqueue kiye ja sakte hain.

Example:

```apex
for (...) {
    System.enqueueJob(...);
}
```

Agar loop mein 100 jobs enqueue karne ki koshish karoge, problem ho sakti hai.

---

# 28. Asynchronous transaction mein limit

Agar tum already asynchronous context mein ho, jaise:

```text
Batch Apex
```

to generally ek transaction mein sirf:

```text
1 Queueable job
```

enqueue kar sakte ho.

Ye difference yaad rakho:

```text
Synchronous transaction
→ up to 50 Queueables

Asynchronous transaction
→ 1 Queueable
```

---

# 29. `Limits.getQueueableJobs()`

Current transaction mein kitne Queueable jobs enqueue ho chuke hain, ye check karne ke liye:

```apex
Limits.getQueueableJobs()
```

use kar sakte ho.

Ye especially useful hai jab dynamic code mein jobs enqueue kar rahe ho.

---

# 30. Queueable chaining ka special rule

Ek Queueable execute ho raha hai:

```text
Parent Queueable
```

Uske andar:

```apex
System.enqueueJob(new ChildQueueable());
```

Allowed hai.

But:

```apex
System.enqueueJob(new Child1());
System.enqueueJob(new Child2());
```

same executing Queueable se multiple children create karna supported nahi hai.

Think:

```text
       Parent
          |
        Child
          |
        Child
          |
        Child
```

not:

```text
       Parent
       /    \
   Child1   Child2
```

---

# 31. Developer/Trial Edition mein stack-depth limitation

Provided documentation ke according Developer aur Trial organizations mein chained Queueables ke liye default maximum stack depth:

```text
5
```

hai.

Meaning chain roughly:

```text
Job 1
 ↓
Job 2
 ↓
Job 3
 ↓
Job 4
 ↓
Job 5
```

tak.

Lekin newer org configurations/other editions mein stack-depth behavior ko `AsyncOptions` se control kiya ja sakta hai.

Isliye production architecture mein exact org/edition limits verify karna important hai.

---

# 32. Queueable job ka execution daily async limit mein count hota hai

Queueable ka execution asynchronous Apex ke shared limits ke against count hota hai.

Simple idea:

```text
Queueable #1 → consumes async execution
Queueable #2 → consumes async execution
Queueable #3 → consumes async execution
```

Agar unnecessarily thousands of Queueables chain karoge, async execution limits consume ho sakti hain.

Isi reason se Salesforce documentation delayed chaining aur safeguards recommend karti hai.

---

# 33. Duplicate Queueable Jobs

Salesforce mein duplicate Queueable jobs ko detect karne ka mechanism bhi available hai.

Idea:

```text
Same signature
      ↓
Queueable Job A
      ↓
Same signature Job B
      ↓
DuplicateMessageException
```

Iska purpose hai unnecessary duplicate async work ko prevent karna.

Ye especially useful ho sakta hai jab multiple transactions same logical async work enqueue karne ki koshish kar rahe hon.

---

# 34. Transaction rollback ka important concept

Ye point bahut important hai.

Suppose:

```apex
System.enqueueJob(new MyQueueable());

insert account;
```

Aur baad mein transaction rollback ho gaya.

Then queued Queueable bhi process nahi hogi.

Simple:

```text
Main Transaction
       ↓
enqueue Queueable
       ↓
Later error
       ↓
Transaction Rollback
       ↓
Queueable NOT processed
```

Why?

Because Queueable enqueueing bhi current transaction ke outcome se associated hai.

---

# 35. Transaction Finalizer

Queueable ka advanced feature hai:

> **Transaction Finalizer**

Ye asynchronous Queueable job ke saath final actions attach karne deta hai.

Particularly useful:

```text
Queueable
   ↓
Processing
   ↓
Failure
   ↓
Finalizer
   ↓
Recovery / Logging / Cleanup
```

Example use cases:

* Error logging
* Retry logic
* Status update
* Cleanup
* Failure handling

Isko Queueable ke "failure recovery mechanism" ke roop mein soch sakte ho.

